ch is that what  we're in your seat you're definitely  seeing a revaluation in some companies  you're seeing a massive revaluation  that's not saying that we can't go back  up to the old valuations but the market  is clearly taking a pause and trying to  stand the long term viability and the  long term wealth creation vehicle for  shareholders your boss said earlier  today that you're seeing pent up IPO  demand because private funding is drying  up our company is really thinking about  going public in a market as volatile as  this well the premise in many of these  technology companies for years has been  growth growth growth and we will be able  to get funding no matter what as long as  we're growing the will use this funding  to grow which will allow us to get the  next round of funding which will allow  us to grow if you can't get funding  it changes the premise to some extent  you now have to think about changing  your model from just pure growth to  creating cash flow to create creating  revenue creating return on capital if  you can create cash flow return on  capital you will have access to the  public market and buyers and investors  will show up at that point historically  companies have not wanted to go into the  public markets because the public  markets will at some point like we're  seeing now they will demand earnings  power they will demand some return on  capital they will demand that you  actually create revenue with that you  just don't grow your company forever so  what happens then to IPO volume in 2016  what happens to MA  how does 2016 compared to 2015 so if  companies choose to are forced to go  public we will see a pickup in the  amount of IPOs but if they're forced to  go public the question is will the  market allow them to go public only  companies with a robust long duration  business model will be able to access  the public markets the public markets  have become very discerning in January  we did not have an IPO exactly we didn't  have 